          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThank you for sharing that. Coronary artery disease is a condition where plaque builds up in the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. According to the General clinical reference summary \"Coronary artery disease\" (condition-coronary-artery-disease), there are two main ways it causes problems:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing** can cause chest discomfort that comes on predictably with effort, which is called stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture** can cause a sudden clot that blocks the artery. This can happen even at a spot that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms, so a normal stress test in the past does not rule out a heart attack now.\n\nThe passage also says that plaque builds up over decades. The sources I searched did not cover treatment, medicines, daily management, or what symptoms should prompt urgent care for this condition. For those questions, a cardiologist or your own clinician is the right person to ask.\n\n**If you have chest pain, pressure, or tightness that is new, worsening, or lasts more than a few minutes, or if you have shortness of breath, fainting, or sweating with it, call your local emergency number now.** Do not wait to see if it passes.\n\nSome questions you might bring to your clinician: what my specific risk is, which symptoms mean I should seek urgent care, and what changes to my daily routine could help.",
