          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is meant by secondary prevention?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nSecondary prevention is the set of measures used for someone who already has established coronary artery disease. This includes a previous heart attack, a procedure to open or bypass an artery, or disease seen on an imaging test. The goal is to lower the chance of another serious event. The \"Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis\" guidance (guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention) describes it this way.\n\nThe General clinical reference summary \"Coronary artery disease\" (condition-coronary-artery-disease) adds that secondary prevention after a diagnosis substantially reduces the rate of further events. It says the benefit comes from a combination of measures rather than any single one.\n\nThe same summary lists common contributors to coronary disease that can be changed, including smoking, high blood pressure, high LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, and diet. Age, male sex, and family history cannot be changed.\n\nThe sources I searched did not say which specific measures make up the package for any individual person, what targets apply, or how often follow-up should happen for you. Your cardiologist or clinician can tell you which parts of secondary prevention apply to your situation.\n\nIf you have new or worsening chest pain, pressure, or tightness, or shortness of breath, fainting, or sweating with it, call your local emergency number now.",
